INT. FORWARD BUNKER - BELGIAN FARMLAND - DAWN

Sandbags lined with straw. A paraffin stove smokes in the corner. A radio set squats on an ammunition crate beside a tin of biscuits. Outside, a pale mist drifts over silent fields.

CAPTAIN MARIANA ESCOBAR, forties, sits on a crate cleaning a pistol with a rag. SERGEANT FITZROY BELL, fifties, scratches at his stubble. CORPORAL YASIR KARIMI, twenties, sleeps against a wall with his boots on. PRIVATE LENNART SKOG, nineteen, stares through the firing slit at the grey horizon.

MARIANA
Fitz, anything on the net?

FITZROY
Static, ma'am. Nothing but.

MARIANA
Quiet is good.

LENNART
Quiet is strange, Captain.

MARIANA
Private, it's dawn. Let the war have its tea.

Fitzroy chuckles. He pries open the biscuit tin and passes it around. Yasir wakes, accepts one, and crunches it. The stove ticks.

MAJOR TREMAINE (V.O.)
(over radio, crackling)
Bunker Seven, this is Command. Do you read?

Everyone freezes. Mariana lunges for the handset.

MARIANA
Command, this is Seven. Reading you.

MAJOR TREMAINE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Captain, the armistice has been signed. Hostilities cease at eleven hundred.

Silence. Yasir lowers the biscuit. Lennart turns from the slit.

FITZROY
(softly)
Eleven.

MARIANA
Say again, Command.

MAJOR TREMAINE (V.O.)
It's over, Captain. It's over.

Mariana sets the handset down. She looks at the others, one by one. Nobody speaks. Then, at the slit, Lennart begins to laugh, and cannot stop.
